\begin{pmatrix}
15 \\
51
\end{pmatrix}
\] The inverse of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix \(\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix}\) is \(\frac{1}{ad - bc} \begin{pmatrix} d & -b \\ -c & a \end{pmatrix}\). So, the inverse of \(\begin{pmatrix} 12 & 21 \\ 21 & 12 \end{pmatrix}\) is \(\frac{1}{12 \cdot 12 - 21 \cdot 21} \begin{pmatrix} 12 & -21 \\ -21 & 12 \end{pmatrix} = \frac{1}{144 - 441} \begin{pmatrix} 12 & -21 \\ -21 & 12 \end{pmatrix} = \frac{1}{-297} \begin{pmatrix} 12 & -21 \\ -21 & 12 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -12/297 & 21/297 \\ 21/297 & -12/297 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -4/99 & 7/99 \\ 7/99 & -4/99 \end{pmatrix}\). Therefore, \[
